title: Ürgüplü's Government Is Rejected
subtitle: May 13, 1972
new-page: true
max-visits: 1

= Ürgüplü's Government Is Rejected

After nearly two weeks of negotiations with the political parties, Suat Hayri Ürgüplü has finally managed to assemble a proposed government with the support of the CHP, AP, MGP and DP. Having successfully brought together parties with vastly different political views, Ürgüplü presents his proposed cabinet and government program to President Cevdet Sunay, expecting to bring the political crisis to an end. However, Ürgüplü has plans of his own. Rather than simply continuing the military-backed administrations of the past year, he intends to restore parliamentary government, prepare the country for early elections and bring the March 12 period to a close. These intentions are far from what Sunay and the military leadership had expected when they entrusted Ürgüplü with forming a government.

Upon examining Ürgüplü's proposed cabinet and government program, Sunay refuses to approve them, effectively vetoing the government before it can even take office. The President considers the proposed administration incompatible with the objectives of the March 12 Memorandum, while the military remains unwilling to surrender its influence over the country's political affairs. Ürgüplü, despite his considerable political experience and the parliamentary support he has gathered, finds himself unable to overcome the President's objections. Rather than abandon his intentions and attempt to form another government more acceptable to the military, he returns the mandate to Sunay, bringing his brief attempt to resolve the government crisis to an unsuccessful end. Once again, the question of who will govern Turkey is left unanswered.

